"""Four authored primitive lessons; evidence read from actual paired harnesses."""
from lesson_writer import author,CPP

def ev(detail):
 def f(link):return detail+'\n\nThe '+link(CPP/'tools/differential.py','primitive differential driver')+' compiles the pinned Java classes and runs '+link(CPP/'support/PrimitiveOracle.java','PrimitiveOracle')+' against '+link(CPP/'support/primitives.cpp','minima_primitives')+'. Both report observable text and wire bytes; both flatten exceptions to `ERROR`. The inspected '+link(CPP/'build/differential-results.json','existing report')+' records 3,802/3,802 comparisons with zero failures for the complete primitive corpus. This does not prove exact error classes, all methods, or full-node parity.'
 return f

def w(name,purpose,steps,adapt,tests,exercise,limits):
 author('src/org/minima/objects/base/'+name+'.md',purpose,steps,adapt,ev(tests),exercise,limits+' The source-map status remains partial and parity not_verified.')

w('MiniByte','A single stored byte with two different numeric views: unsigned for protocol fields, signed when matching Java byte values.',[
 'The integer constructor casts to Java byte, retaining only the low eight bits. The boolean constructor produces exactly 0 or 1.',
 'getValue masks with 0xFF and therefore returns 0 through 255. getByteValue returns the signed byte, -128 through 127. The same stored bits can consequently print as 255 or -1 depending on the getter.',
 'Equality compares the unsigned values. isFalse means exactly zero; every other byte, including 2 and 255, is true.',
 'Serialization writes exactly one byte. Reading consumes one byte with no separate length prefix; toString uses the unsigned value.'
], 'Native storage is uint8_t. The int constructor performs the same narrowing, and bit_cast<int8_t> provides the signed view without relying on implementation-defined signed conversion. This reuses the established primitive counterpart documented in its source header.',
 'The driver tests every integer from -512 through 512, comparing unsigned text, signed getter and wire byte. It therefore traverses the byte range repeatedly and exercises wraparound. It does not directly call isTrue/isFalse, so those semantics here come from reading the bodies.',
 'Explain why MiniByte(257) has unsigned value 1 and why MiniByte(255).isTrue is true even though its signed value is -1.',
 'Boolean truth is not restricted to the canonical serialized values 0 and 1. Callers that need canonical validation must perform it separately.')
w('MiniString','The UTF-8 text wrapper used by Minima serialization. It deliberately decodes and re-encodes data, so malformed byte sequences are normalized.',[
 'The Java String constructor first encodes UTF-8, then delegates to the byte constructor, which decodes UTF-8. Invalid surrogate/text input can therefore be replaced during construction.',
 'The byte constructor decodes with Java UTF-8 replacement behavior. getData encodes the normalized stored string again; it does not retain the original malformed bytes.',
 'isEqual compares text. The copy constructor copies the other wrapper’s string.',
 'Serialization wraps getData in MiniData, giving a four-byte big-endian byte count followed by UTF-8 bytes. Reading decodes a MiniData payload back to text.',
 'The count is bytes rather than Java UTF-16 code units or visible characters. A panda emoji occupies four UTF-8 bytes although it is one Unicode code point.'
], 'C++ stores UTF-8 in std::string and normalizes with the shared UTF8Spans decoder. Each invalid span becomes U+FFFD encoded as EF BF BD. Native length and subset helpers operate on byte positions and are additional APIs, not direct counterparts of Java String.length or substring.',
 'The fixture includes empty text, ASCII, euro and panda characters; every individual byte; 2,000 seeded random byte sequences; and explicit malformed surrogate, overlong, out-of-range and truncated UTF-8 sequences. It compares the reserialized normalized bytes. These are byte-input tests, not exhaustive tests of every possible Java String constructor input.',
 'Predict the length prefix for the panda emoji. Why can serializing malformed input produce more bytes than were originally supplied?',
 'Java exposes MINIMA_CHARSET as a mutable static Charset; the native wrapper is fixed to UTF-8. Charset mutation and byte-subset extension behavior are outside the reported primitive fixture.')
w('MiniData','A byte sequence that also supports unsigned numeric interpretation. Its byte identity and its numerical ordering are deliberately different concepts.',[
 'Decode hexadecimal through BaseConverter. A byte-array constructor stores the supplied Java array reference; the minimum-length constructor pads on the left with zero bytes only when needed.',
 'The nonnegative BigInteger constructor removes a redundant positive sign byte. Numeric zero is represented by one zero byte, whereas the default constructor is an empty sequence.',
 'isEqual requires identical lengths and bytes. Ordering converts to a positive BigInteger, so 0x01 and 0x0001 have equal numeric magnitude despite unequal byte identity.',
 'Java lazily caches that BigInteger. getBytes exposes the original mutable array, so later byte mutation can disagree with an already cached numeric view. This is an ownership behavior, not just a performance detail.',
 'Shifts operate on the positive numeric magnitude and rebuild bytes; they can discard leading zero padding. Concatenation instead preserves the exact left and right sequences.',
 'Normal wire format is a four-byte signed length followed by bytes. The reader enforces the 512 MiB maximum and optional exact expected length. A negative ordinary length reaches Java array allocation; hash reading explicitly rejects negative lengths and limits length to 64.',
 'The Streamable convenience converter serializes the object and returns null after logging IOException. Random data uses SecureRandom; the demonstration main prints random and padded values and is not a unit test.'
], 'C++ owns a vector and exposes const byte access, so it does not reproduce Java’s externally mutable array alias/cache behavior. Numeric comparison skips leading zero bytes rather than constructing BigInteger. OS randomness comes from /dev/urandom on the current POSIX target. Native SHA-256 and SHA3-256 helpers are extra implementation support; they are not methods of this Java class.',
 'The primitive fixture compares empty, zero, padded, odd-length and invalid hex inputs, plus numeric less-than cases such as 0x01 versus 0x0001. It establishes selected parsing, formatting and normal-wire behavior. It does not directly test Java mutable-array aliasing, all shift distances, random quality, the convenience converter, or the 512 MiB allocation boundary.',
 'Can two MiniData objects compare neither less nor greater yet fail isEqual? Give a concrete byte example, then explain how shifting by zero can change its byte representation.',
 'Several Java convenience APIs, including getDataValueDecimal, compare, shortened to0xString and the Streamable converter, are not represented as matching methods in the inspected native header. Equality against an arbitrary Java Object also has cast/null behavior absent from the typed C++ interface.')
w('MiniNumber','A decimal number with bounded constructor range, controlled precision and a scale-sensitive wire representation. It is used for amounts, counters and KISS arithmetic.',[
 'Think of the value as unscaled_integer × 10^(-scale). Java BigDecimal supplies that pair and rounds to 64 significant digits with RoundingMode.DOWN, meaning toward zero rather than toward negative infinity.',
 'Constructors call checkLimits: first truncate scales above 44 decimal places, then reject values outside ±(2^64 - 1). isValidMinimaValue adds a different rule: greater than zero and at most one billion.',
 'Arithmetic uses the same math context and builds a new MiniNumber. Addition/subtraction align decimal scales; multiplication combines them; division must keep sufficient significant digits before truncation. Remainder follows Java remainder semantics, not always a nonnegative mathematical modulo.',
 'floor and ceil deliberately use FLOOR and CEILING, unlike general DOWN rounding. setSignificantDigits rejects values below 0 or above 64; zero is accepted because MathContext(0) means unlimited precision before the result returns through normal construction.',
 'Numeric comparisons ignore scale. toString removes trailing zeros and prints plain decimal notation, while decimalPlaces returns the stored scale. Thus 1 and 1.00 can print identically but retain distinct binary encodings.',
 'Wire output stores signed one-byte scale, one-byte byte-array length, then the signed two’s-complement BigInteger bytes. Positive 128 needs a leading 00 sign byte; negative values use signed encoding.',
 'The reader accepts signed scale, rejects lengths below 0 or above 32, reads the BigInteger and applies the 64-digit math context. It does not call checkLimits. A value arriving from bytes can therefore violate constructor range or decimal-place restrictions until another operation reconstructs it.',
 'Integer conversions truncate fractions and use Java narrowing semantics for overflow. They are not checked range conversions; this matters when a decimal controls an index or loop.'
], 'Native MiniNumber explicitly stores a BigInt and scale. Its BigInt implements signed byte normalization, magnitude addition/subtraction, schoolbook multiplication and long division whose next base-256 quotient digit is found by binary search. Decimal addition aligns scales, exponentiation uses repeated squaring with guard digits, and sqrt uses an integer Newton iteration on a scaled radicand. Static deserialization intentionally bypasses constructor limits. This is substantially more than replacing BigDecimal with double.',
 'Selected constants include range endpoints, excessive precision, negative values and differently scaled zeros. The driver adds 100 seeded groups of add/subtract/multiply inputs, division including zero, floor/ceil, significant-digit zero, integer narrowing, square roots and positive/negative powers. Each numeric result compares both text and wire bytes. Although the harness supports mod, this driver does not generate a mod case, so its branch is not direct modulo coverage.',
 'Show why floor(-1.2) is -2 but truncation toward zero is -1. Then encode the conceptual pairs for 1 and 1.00 and explain why equal comparison does not force equal wire bytes.',
 'Division now uses JavaArithmeticException: 0/0 reports Division undefined, while nonzero/0 reports Division by zero. Seven supplemental observations in NumberValueOriginalTests compare exact class/message text for 0, 1 and -1 over both 0 and 0.000, plus zero to power -1; both inspected normal and sanitized reports pass alongside 99 original NumberValue assertions. Negative square root and invalid significant digits still use generic standard exceptions in the inspected bodies. Generic primitive ERROR comparisons cannot establish their Java error parity. The native header also lacks matching exposed MAXIMUM and BigDecimal-returning APIs; exhaustive API parity remains open.')
